Diabetic Nephropathy
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Long-standing diabetes mellitus, Frothy urine/proteinuria, Peripheral pitting oedema, Elevated urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-011
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of lower limb swelling and breathlessness in the context of known diabetes, requiring urgent evaluation to rule out acute pulmonary oedema.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed rapidly progressive peripheral oedema and new-onset exertional dyspnoea over 48 hours. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to evaluate for impending fluid overload and severe hypertensive crisis. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a high risk of respiratory failure secondary to acute pulmonary oedema.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs are ballooning up, my urine is full of foam, and I can barely walk to the bathroom without losing my breath."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms escalated progressively over the past week. The patient, who has a long-standing history of poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus, noted a rapid increase in bilateral peripheral pitting oedema. They report passing frothy urine, indicating significant macroscopic proteinuria. This is accompanied by a gradual onset of generalized fatigue, profound lethargy, and new-onset dyspnoea on minimal exertion. They deny chest pain but report a recent exacerbation of established hypertension, confirmed via home monitoring. Nausea and profound anorexia have severely limited oral intake over the last 48 hours. Nocturia due to osmotic diuresis and progressive renal impairment has caused severe sleep disruption.
Functional Status: Mobilizing short distances only due to dyspnoea and heavy oedematous legs.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to fatigue; lives alone and struggles with medication adherence.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 185/95 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 22 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 94 Kg
General: Pale, visibly fatigued, and exhibiting mild respiratory distress on exertion.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Hypertensive, regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs. Respiratory: Fine bibasal crepitations on auscultation. Extremities: 3+ peripheral pitting oedema bilaterally extending to the mid-calves. Expected investigation results will show an persistently elevated urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and a slowly declining estimated glomerular filtration rate.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty takeaway containers; poorly managed medication pillbox with missed doses of antihypertensives.
Medication Reconciliation: Poor adherence to prescribed ACE inhibitors and insulin regimen confirmed.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Diabetic Nephropathy - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary solitary cause due to the prominent frothy urine indicating massive proteinuria, pointing to a primary renal protein-losing pathology.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bilateral, symmetrical nature of the severe pitting oedema.

Liver Cirrhosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of ascites, jaundice, or other stigmata of chronic liver disease.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of volume overload secondary to diabetic nephropathy, evidenced by massive proteinuria, severe hypertension, and early pulmonary oedema. The immediate risk of progressive respiratory failure or hypertensive emergency is high. Prompt after-hours physical examination was essential to auscultate the lung fields, quantify the degree of fluid overload, and aggressively optimize diuretic and antihypertensive therapy. Deferring this assessment until regular clinic hours would have exposed the patient to an unacceptable risk of life-threatening acute pulmonary oedema or cardiovascular collapse, entirely satisfying the criteria for an urgent MBS after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral furosemide 40mg stat to initiate diuresis. Reinstated ACE inhibitor therapy immediately. Ordered urgent fasting bloods and formal urine protein quantification for the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To encourage medication adherence, the patient is instructed to link taking their morning medications with their daily habit of boiling the kettle for tea. Consistently taking the tablets at this established cue will build automaticity, and observing a subsequent reduction in leg swelling will strongly reinforce the routine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Domiciliary nurse referral for medication administration support.
DME/Safety (Optional): Shower chair recommended to conserve energy and prevent falls.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review tomorrow afternoon to assess diuretic response and respiratory status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer via 000 is required if the patient develops shortness of breath at rest, severe chest tightness, or if they become completely unable to pass urine.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The current symptoms represent a severe strain on the kidneys requiring strict, ongoing specialist management.
Clinical Course: Diuretic therapy should begin to reduce the leg swelling over the next 24 hours, but blood pressure requires careful titration over the coming weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you experience sudden difficulty breathing while lying flat, or if you feel a heavy crushing sensation in your chest, you must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The diabetes has damaged the filters in the kidneys, causing protein to leak out and fluid to build up in the body. The water pill will force the kidneys to excrete this excess fluid, relieving the pressure on the lungs and legs.
Actionable Steps: Take the water pill early in the day to prevent nighttime urination. Restrict dietary sodium strictly. Adhere perfectly to the blood pressure medications to protect remaining kidney function.

